'Correct. Two candidate causes (wine or rusty pipe); evidence eliminates one (the pipe is sound); the other is concluded to be the cause — the same causal-elimination structure with new subject matter.',
'This asserts one cause (traffic) without eliminating the other (the alarm) — affirming a candidate is not the same as ruling out its rival. This partial structural match is the half-right trap.',
'A trend about frost timing is topically related but performs no causal elimination at all. True or not, it does not reproduce the reasoning: the irrelevant-true trap.',
'This concludes the pesticide was defective without any evidence eliminating the frost — the rival cause is never addressed. Concluding without the elimination step is the wrong-conclusion trap.',
