    explanationWalkthrough:
'This is a demanding abstraction because the negative quantifier ("no") interacts with the universal ("all") in a specific order: the exclusion applies to the larger category (reptiles), and the subset (lizards) inherits the exclusion. Form: No A are B; all C are A; therefore no C are B. Choice (A) preserves the order: no commercial flights allow smoking (exclusion on the large category); all red-eye flights are commercial flights (subset); therefore no red-eye flights allow smoking. Choice (B) is the engineered near-miss: it is valid and looks similar, but it chains two exclusions (no A are B; all B are C; so no A are C) — a different arrangement of the terms, making it the half-right trap. Choice (C) reverses the inference: from "no lizards are warm-blooded" and "all lizards are reptiles" it concludes "no reptiles are warm-blooded" — but the exclusion cannot be projected upward from subset to superset; that is the wrong-conclusion trap. Choice (D) weakens "no" to "most" in the first premise while keeping the absolute "no" conclusion — the quantifier no longer licenses the inference: the too-strong trap. Choice (E) is a true biological fact with no argument structure at all — the irrelevant-true trap.',
